            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Algebraic Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "83"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.108,
            0.884,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "We know that the volume of this cube is $( a + b ) ^ { 3 }$ . Let us split this cube into smaller cubes and cuboids."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.155,
            0.925,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.325,
                0.71,
                0.673,
                0.728
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.10: Representation of the identity"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.728,
            0.625,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "( a + b ) ^ { 3 } = a ^ { 3 } + 3 a ^ { 2 } b + 3 a b ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 3 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.756,
            0.884,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "Notice that the larger cube can be split into two cubes and six cuboids. The cubes have volumes, $a ^ { 3 }$ and $b ^ { \\overline { { 3 } } }$ cubic units, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.795,
            0.885,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "Amongst the other six cuboids, three have dimensions a units × a units $\\times \\textit { b }$ units and other three have dimensions a units $\\times b$ units × b units, and so their volumes are $a ^ { 2 } b$ and $a b ^ { 2 }$ cubic units, respectively. Hence the total volume of the six cuboids is $3 a ^ { 2 } b ^ { \\mathrm { ~ + ~ } } 3 a b ^ { 2 }$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
